    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About</h1>

      <p>
        I am an AI engineer. I build production pipelines that use language models
        for narrow, repetitive work, and I spend most of my time on the parts that
        decide whether such a system survives contact with real volume: whether the
        output validates, how often it has to be retried, and what it costs per
        successful result.
      </p>

      <h2>The argument I keep making</h2>

      <p>
        A lot of shipped LLM features are a frontier API behind a prompt. That works
        until volume arrives, and then the unit economics decide the feature's
        future rather than the roadmap does. The interesting engineering is in the
        middle: constrained decoding instead of retries, a small tuned model for the
        narrow task, a deterministic solver for the parts that were never a language
        problem, and an escalation path to a large model only for the inputs that
        need one.
      </p>

      <p>
        The answer is rarely all API or all self-hosted. It depends on volume,
        latency tolerance, and what a wrong answer costs. I'd rather publish the
        threshold than pick a side.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm doing right now</h2>

      <p>
        Building one system at a time and taking each through three stages — a
        frontier API, self-hosted open weights, then a fine-tune — scoring every
        stage against the same held-out set. The writeups here carry the
        architecture, the numbers, and the failures. Code is on GitHub.
      </p>

      <h2>Working together</h2>

      <p>
        If you have an LLM feature that works but costs more than it earns, or one
        that returns malformed output often enough that someone is patching it by
        hand, that is the problem I most like to be handed. Email is the fastest way
        to reach me.
      </p>
    </article>
